<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Soloi</h1><p class="st-meta">1000 BCE – 312 BCE (approx.) · capital Soloi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Cypriot kingdom on Morphou Bay, said to have taken its name from the Athenian lawgiver Solon, who advised its king.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Soloi lay on the bay of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morphou">Morphou</a> in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skouriotissa">Skouriotissa</a>. Founded in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, it was a city-kingdom by the 7th century BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> tells that King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philocyprus">Philocyprus</a> moved the city to a better site on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solon">Solon's</a> advice and named it after him.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Soloi grew rich on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and farmland. Its king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristocyprus">Aristocyprus</a> fell fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamis-cyprus">Salamis</a> in 497 BCE, after which Soloi endured a five-month siege. The palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palace-of-vouni">Vouni</a> on a nearby hill, built in the 5th century BCE, may have been used to watch over Soloi. King Pasicrates supported Alexander and staged a dramatic contest for him at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tyre">Tyre</a> in 331 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Its last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunostos-of-soloi">Eunostos</a>, married Eirene, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I</a>, and the kingdom was absorbed into Ptolemaic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> c. 312 BCE. Soloi remained a city into Roman and early Christian times.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1000 BCE – 312 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:39.80%;width:13.69%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1000 BCE – 312 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Soloi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Philocyprus, Aristocyprus, Eunostos</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Cypriot Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arcado-Cypriot Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/cyprus">Cyprus</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Soloi, Soli</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-656&amp;focus=kingdom-of-soloi" aria-label="Kingdom of Soloi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5511 907 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Soloi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5831 1163L6140 1251M5831 1163L5772 1297"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6140 1251h0M5772 1297h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5831 1163h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5831 1163h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Soloi. <a href="/atlas?year=-656&amp;focus=kingdom-of-soloi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

